<div class="mb-12 rounded-lg border border-gray-200 bg-gray-50 p-6" data-reveal>
  <h2 class="mb-5 text-[26px] font-semibold leading-[1.2] tracking-tight text-[#111827] md:text-[30px]">Frequently asked questions</h2>
  <div class="mt-6 space-y-6">
    <div><p class="mb-2 text-[17px] font-medium text-[#111827]">What is predictive maintenance?</p><p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">It uses sensors on machines, such as vibration and temperature, and a model that learns what normal looks like, so signs of failure can be flagged before a breakdown. It sits between fixing things when they break and servicing them on a fixed schedule.</p></div>
    <div><p class="mb-2 text-[17px] font-medium text-[#111827]">How accurate is AI at predicting machine failures?</p><p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">A January 2026 review of 85 studies in Frontiers in Mechanical Engineering reports roughly 85% to 94% accuracy for fault classification and downtime reductions of 15% to 40%. These are averages across many studies and sites, not a promise for any one plant.</p></div>
    <div><p class="mb-2 text-[17px] font-medium text-[#111827]">Can a small factory use predictive maintenance?</p><p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">Possibly, but the evidence is thinner. A 2016 NIST pilot survey found small and medium manufacturers behind large ones on advanced maintenance. A sensible start is one critical machine, a few sensors and a written failure log.</p></div>
    <div><p class="mb-2 text-[17px] font-medium text-[#111827]">What is the biggest practical problem?</p><p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">Data quality and false alarms. The same review names data quality, computing cost, interpretability, generalisation and integration as the main challenges, and a 2021 heavy-industry study was built specifically to cut false alarms.</p></div>
  </div>
</div>

